{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795213917,"nonce":"KgPfefvP43m0/wSRbipadBWDafA=","message":"Comparison on the x86_64 NUC (16 cores, release build of c4eeb6c2, default Cranelift tiers). Default pool collector against ZYNTAX_HOST_HEAP=immix, interleaved runs, min/median/max of wall time and peak RSS. No rayzor or ash work was running; load stayed near 2 with the benchmark itself running. Output is identical between the two heaps on the smoke run, and no run failed.\n\nMedian wall, Immix over pool, and median peak RSS:\n\n| Workload | Pool | Immix | Ratio | RSS pool / Immix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| tok2 (gmatch count loop) | 1.86 s | 2.45 s | 1.32 | 226 / 200 MB |\n| w_grow (3M-entry table) | 0.32 s | 0.39 s | 1.22 | 638 / 613 MB |\n| w_hold (1.5M live, churn) | 1.69 s | 2.34 s | 1.38 | 1610 / 1513 MB |\n| w_phase (churn, then build) | 1.53 s | 2.59 s | 1.69 | 643 / 608 MB |\n| pipeline | 0.26 s | 0.26 s | 1.00 | 152 / 156 MB |\n| Lua binary_trees | 0.15 s | 0.20 s | 1.33 | 69 / 69 MB |\n| records | 0.08 s | 0.09 s | 1.13 | 121 / 121 MB |\n| tokenize | 0.09 s | 0.11 s | 1.22 | 76 / 79 MB |\n| Python binary_trees | 0.34 s | 0.52 s | 1.53 | 74 / 71 MB |\n\nSpreads are tight (under 10% min to max on every row), so these are not noise.\n\nThe gap is wider here than on the Mac, where Immix was 0 to 27% slower. The worst rows are w_phase and Python binary_trees. The pool arm honours the compiler's explicit releases and Immix ignores them (free is a no-op inside the heap), so every object the drop analysis would have released goes through marking and sweeping instead. That is the likely cause of the gap but I have not isolated it on this machine. Peak RSS is 4 to 12% lower with Immix on the large rows and level on the small ones.\n\nNext: profile w_phase and Python binary_trees on x86 to split the gap between mark cost and the allocation path, before tuning either.","files":null}]}